WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.792 --> 00:00:02.281
- [Gary] Well thanks for coming.

00:00:02.281 --> 00:00:04.994
I would love to 
answer any questions,

00:00:04.994 --> 00:00:06.506
if you guys 
wanna do as much Q&amp;amp;A

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:09.071
and then maybe take a 
couple selfies, hug it out.

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:10.667
(audience laughter)

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:14.441
And then I'll get 
mentally prepared for my talk.

00:00:14.441 --> 00:00:15.873
Anybody have any questions?

00:00:15.873 --> 00:00:18.560
- [Man] Yeah.
- [Gary] Go ahead.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.646
As someone 
transitioning straight out of

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.940
a lot of finance 
and stuff like that,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:23.994
coming into the tech world,

00:00:23.994 --> 00:00:27.958
how much technical 
expertise do you need

00:00:27.958 --> 00:00:30.541
versus becoming 
a jack of all trades

00:00:30.541 --> 00:00:33.617
and learning bits and 
bobs from where you can?

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:34.984
- [Gary] I think it's 
predicated on the ambition

00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:36.954
and the life cycle 
of where you're at. Right?

00:00:36.954 --> 00:00:38.220
The way I think about business

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.543
is actually the way 
I think about parenting.

00:00:40.543 --> 00:00:42.182
I don't expect a three year old

00:00:42.182 --> 00:00:45.545
to have everything 
figured out yet.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.217
I think it depends 
on the makeup of a team.

00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:48.294
You look at a team like this

00:00:48.294 --> 00:00:50.743
and everybody's gonna have
their strengths and weaknesses.

00:00:50.743 --> 00:00:52.344
The one thing I do see a lot of,

00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:56.473
because I'm a little bit more
of a hustler, entrepreneur.

00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:57.967
I wasn't a developer.

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:01.186
I came up from selling 
stuff and then a wine store

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:04.690
and so I get a lot of 
hustlers and entrepreneurs

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.234
that come to me that 
wanna be in the tech business

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:08.777
and they come up 
with these ideas

00:01:08.777 --> 00:01:11.238
and then they want a
dev shop to build it for them,

00:01:11.238 --> 00:01:12.489
and they're gonna 
launch this huge...

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:15.990
We're gonna be the next
Facebook because of this idea.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.662
And then I go, 
you're in the tech business

00:01:18.662 --> 00:01:21.331
and you don't have 
a tech cofounder.

00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:23.208
I see that every day.

00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:26.295
I think if the 
ambition is very large

00:01:26.295 --> 00:01:31.008
and it's tech-centric, you
need to have a tech cofounder

00:01:31.008 --> 00:01:33.302
to really build an 
actual big business.

00:01:33.302 --> 00:01:36.638
I think if you're 
building a fashion brand

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:39.016
and there's products 
like Magento and Shopify

00:01:39.016 --> 00:01:41.310
and WordPress 
already in the marketplace

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.314
and your ambition is to build
a $10 million scarf business,

00:01:45.314 --> 00:01:47.566
well then you don't need
to be the tech cofounder.

00:01:47.566 --> 00:01:50.110
There's tools that can 
get you there through Shopify

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.945
and maybe it's more 
about the marketing skills,

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:54.698
or how do you 
market the crayons?

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.160
I think you 
have to go backwards.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.079
I think the thing that 
everybody makes mistakes with,

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:04.249
I think one of the best 
things I've ever done...

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:06.658
I articulated about 
my love of the journey

00:02:06.658 --> 00:02:09.755
but I don't talk enough 
about the way I strategize.

00:02:09.755 --> 00:02:13.245
I kind of like playing the
role of charismatic crazy guy

00:02:13.245 --> 00:02:16.947
but I definitely think 
about stuff quite a bit.

00:02:16.947 --> 00:02:18.584
And I think the best way to win

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:20.891
is to know where 
the hell you're going.

00:02:20.891 --> 00:02:24.227
And the reason I've been
pushing self-awareness

00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:26.563
is not everybody 
here is destined

00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:29.066
to build a $500 million thing.

00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:31.276
I also think it's crazy 
that people don't realize

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:34.404
how amazing it is to 
build a $1 million thing.

00:02:34.404 --> 00:02:37.282
If you can build a $1 million 
a year revenue business

00:02:37.282 --> 00:02:41.703
and run on 30% net profit, 
you have a crazy life.

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:43.288
And so we have this 
whole world of everybody

00:02:43.288 --> 00:02:46.750
trying to achieve for something
that's virtually impossible,

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.086
and they're putting 
themselves in a position where,

00:02:49.086 --> 00:02:51.213
by trying to build 
something impossible,

00:02:51.213 --> 00:02:54.549
they're gonna end 
up working for a bank.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:56.551
Because the one thing that
people haven't factored in yet

00:02:56.551 --> 00:02:58.951
is, globally, the economy
has been pretty good

00:02:58.951 --> 00:03:02.641
for the last decade, 
or at least eight years.

00:03:02.641 --> 00:03:07.145
My biggest concern is 
the next economic slowdown.

00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:09.773
Obviously, Ireland's had 
different things going on

00:03:09.773 --> 00:03:12.422
than the U.K., and 
Brexit for the U.K. and the U.S.

00:03:12.422 --> 00:03:14.264
Everybody has their 
own microeconomic things

00:03:14.264 --> 00:03:16.613
but I think the reality is,

00:03:16.613 --> 00:03:19.991
globally right 
now things are good.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:22.619
People are tense about politics

00:03:22.619 --> 00:03:25.769
but economic realities and

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:27.749
all the people that made money

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:29.876
in the 2000's or 
have family money

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.337
are pouring money 
into startup land.

00:03:32.337 --> 00:03:36.569
A rugby startup can actually
get money from some individual

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:38.468
that made money prior.

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:41.221
The amount of $25,000 
and $50,000 stupid checks

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:44.473
I've written in 
the last five years.

00:03:44.473 --> 00:03:47.125
When that goes away, 
what happens?

00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:51.064
And so I think that's a
long-winded answer

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:51.898
for a couple things.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:55.152
One, you need to know 
what your ambition is. Right?

00:03:55.152 --> 00:03:57.571
Two, you have to figure 
out if what you're doing now

00:03:57.571 --> 00:03:59.408
is just the place 
where you're transitioning

00:03:59.408 --> 00:04:01.032
into the next 
chapter of your career,

00:04:01.032 --> 00:04:04.119
or is this the thing 
that you wanna try to build.

00:04:04.119 --> 00:04:07.247
If you actually decided 
from a strategical standpoint

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.307
that the next two, three years

00:04:08.307 --> 00:04:11.321
is really just about 
learning, less posturing,

00:04:11.321 --> 00:04:13.255
faking it to make it, 
more just learning,

00:04:13.255 --> 00:04:17.007
tasting it, understanding the
grass is not always greener.

00:04:17.007 --> 00:04:18.760
Yeah startup land is 
better than finance land

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.472
'cause people aren't as 
douchey or this and that,

00:04:20.472 --> 00:04:22.721
but there's a lot of 
things that aren't as good.

00:04:22.721 --> 00:04:24.818
Like pressure, 
like it's all on you.

00:04:24.818 --> 00:04:26.808
Like duh-duh-duh-duh-duh.

00:04:26.808 --> 00:04:28.924
Well then if you've 
made the macro decision

00:04:28.924 --> 00:04:30.072
that this is a stepping stone

00:04:30.072 --> 00:04:31.605
and this is how you're 
gonna go about the world,

00:04:31.605 --> 00:04:34.024
all of a sudden you're 
not spending most of your time

00:04:34.024 --> 00:04:36.363
trying to make the 
most money out of this,

00:04:36.363 --> 00:04:38.570
you're taking it in a 
totally different mindset.

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.198
You've almost got a
university-like mentality

00:04:41.198 --> 00:04:42.115
to the execution.

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:43.575
You don't wanna 
go out of business,

00:04:43.575 --> 00:04:47.704
you don't wanna have 
to go back to get a job.

00:04:47.704 --> 00:04:49.122
But I'm giving advice 
to everybody right now.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:52.209
You really have to know what
you're doing, at the macro.

00:04:52.209 --> 00:04:53.710
And the other thing is 
you have to be flexible enough

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.212
to know it changes.

00:04:55.212 --> 00:04:59.007
What your ambition 
is at 19 and 15 and 22

00:04:59.007 --> 00:05:01.426
is different than what 
your ambition is at 41.

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:03.095
You have to be 
flexible in that reality.

00:05:03.095 --> 00:05:05.764
I think it's a strategic answer.

00:05:06.944 --> 00:05:11.953
I think you need to 
think about tasting everything

00:05:12.483 --> 00:05:13.438
because you're a young man

00:05:13.438 --> 00:05:14.736
and you wanna figure 
out what the hell you like

00:05:14.736 --> 00:05:15.821
and what you're good at.

00:05:15.821 --> 00:05:19.366
There's this 
incredible access point

00:05:19.366 --> 00:05:21.410
of what you're great 
at and what you love.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.163
When you stumble into 
being somebody like me,

00:05:26.063 --> 00:05:29.483
you really win when what 
you're naturally great at

00:05:29.483 --> 00:05:32.819
is what you naturally 
love the most, and away you go.

00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:34.613
The amount of people 
that I meet every day

00:05:34.613 --> 00:05:36.948
that are great at something 
but have no interest

00:05:36.948 --> 00:05:38.200
in being a CFO.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.870
They're insane with numbers
but they don't wanna do it.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:43.205
They wanna sing. Right?

00:05:44.992 --> 00:05:46.285
And so that's tough.

00:05:46.285 --> 00:05:48.203
You see it in athletics.

00:05:48.203 --> 00:05:49.955
People don't 
understand athletes.

00:05:49.955 --> 00:05:51.623
The amount of 
athletes that just do it

00:05:51.623 --> 00:05:53.542
because it was the 
best way, in the same way

00:05:53.542 --> 00:05:55.502
that somebody goes 
and becomes an accountant,

00:05:55.502 --> 00:05:57.171
and they don't love competition,

00:05:57.171 --> 00:05:59.089
and they don't love the sport.

00:05:59.089 --> 00:06:01.175
It's crazy when you look at them

00:06:01.175 --> 00:06:03.180
'cause you're like, 
you're one of the 15 best,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.823
and they don't 
love it which is why

00:06:04.823 --> 00:06:06.992
they're not the 
greatest athlete of all-time.

00:06:06.992 --> 00:06:08.618
The greatest 
athletes of all-time

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:11.371
are the ones who 
love it and have it.

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:13.873
And so I think what you need
to do is taste everything.

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:14.913
It's kind of like food.

00:06:14.913 --> 00:06:16.044
You don't know what 
your favorite food is

00:06:16.044 --> 00:06:18.128
if you only eat one food.

00:06:18.128 --> 00:06:19.629
So I would take 
advantage of your youth

00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:23.142
and your flexibility at this age

00:06:23.142 --> 00:06:26.389
and try to taste everything, 
play with everything,

00:06:26.389 --> 00:06:28.108
figure out what 
you might be good at,

00:06:28.108 --> 00:06:31.173
what you might like, and 
then you make a decision.

00:06:31.173 --> 00:06:34.269
When I think 
about being 41 years old

00:06:34.269 --> 00:06:38.063
and feeling as young as I, 
I feel like I'm 10 years old.

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:40.650
I feel like I have my 
whole life in front of me.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.277
And I think it's very difficult

00:06:42.277 --> 00:06:44.279
for youngsters to realize that.

00:06:44.279 --> 00:06:48.450
I'm sure some of us that have
lived a little bit longer...

00:06:49.451 --> 00:06:50.994
How old are you?
- 26.

00:06:50.994 --> 00:06:54.712
- When I was 26, 41 seemed
like a fucking long way away.

00:06:54.712 --> 00:06:56.082
(group laughter)

00:06:56.082 --> 00:07:00.086
My eight years older 
cousin who worked with me

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:02.213
at Wine Library 
seemed old to me.

00:07:02.213 --> 00:07:04.296
He was 34 when I was 26.

00:07:04.296 --> 00:07:05.717
So 41, fuck.

00:07:05.717 --> 00:07:10.263
It's funny for me to think
about how you're seeing me,

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:11.765
but I'm telling you, 
you're gonna feel

00:07:11.765 --> 00:07:15.018
exactly the way you 
feel right now at 41.

00:07:15.018 --> 00:07:16.478
And so that's interesting,

00:07:16.478 --> 00:07:18.396
because when 
people understand that,

00:07:18.396 --> 00:07:21.239
you can start 
deploying more patience.

00:07:21.239 --> 00:07:24.402
Because right now 
you wanna prove it, right?

00:07:24.402 --> 00:07:25.236
Yeah. Quickly.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.155
I can read that because

00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:28.948
first words out of your mouth

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:32.577
always are such an indicator 
of what's on your mind.

00:07:32.577 --> 00:07:35.080
I never cared to 
fake it 'til I made it

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:38.583
because I didn't 
care what people thought.

00:07:40.377 --> 00:07:42.212
When you can eliminate that,

00:07:42.212 --> 00:07:44.839
shit changes real quick.

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:47.676
Even think about high school
and then going into college.

00:07:47.676 --> 00:07:49.312
High school is 
so tough for people

00:07:49.312 --> 00:07:50.887
because it's the apex of when

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:53.640
everything is about 
what other people think.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.934
And then you love the 
transition to university

00:07:55.934 --> 00:08:00.171
or the last year of high school
cause you care a little less.

00:08:00.171 --> 00:08:01.575
Those first couple 
years of high school,

00:08:01.575 --> 00:08:03.566
I don't know the school system 
exactly how you guys do it here

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:08.363
but everybody's wrapped up
in that 12 to 17-year-old age

00:08:08.363 --> 00:08:09.931
of everything is about 
what everybody else thinks

00:08:09.931 --> 00:08:14.411
and that's why so 
many people hate that era.

00:08:14.411 --> 00:08:15.787
I just never went 
through that luckily,

00:08:15.787 --> 00:08:17.041
just hardwiring, 
good parenting,

00:08:17.041 --> 00:08:20.041
serendipity, 
circumstance,

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:23.795
wrapping my self esteem in
things that I was great at.

00:08:23.795 --> 00:08:27.674
I'm not really good at
thinking about what I'm bad at.

00:08:27.674 --> 00:08:29.843
Even in interviews, 
the only time I get stumped

00:08:29.843 --> 00:08:31.761
is they're like, 
"What do you struggle with,"

00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:33.304
or, "What's a failure?"

00:08:33.304 --> 00:08:37.475
I'm like uh, I just 
don't even like, quantify it.

00:08:37.475 --> 00:08:39.561
I don't think it's worth it.

00:08:39.561 --> 00:08:41.980
I think people dwell too much.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:44.181
- So what is the best 
collaboration that either you've

00:08:44.181 --> 00:08:46.735
had as one of your businesses,
or that you've seen happen

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:47.652
in startups?

00:08:47.652 --> 00:08:50.572
- I give away 
all my best advice.

00:08:50.572 --> 00:08:53.742
DailyVee I'm showing inside
meetings to what the strategy

00:08:53.742 --> 00:08:56.119
of my company is, and my
competitors can just watch it

00:08:56.119 --> 00:08:58.830
and copy, I mean it's 
crazy what I'm actually doing,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.515
'cause it speaks to my
confidence that I'm always gonna

00:09:01.515 --> 00:09:04.502
keep inventing the next 
thing and my understanding

00:09:04.502 --> 00:09:05.962
that 99% of people 
aren't gonna do anything

00:09:05.962 --> 00:09:07.547
about it anyway.

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:11.426
So I just bought PureWow, 
which is a women's media

00:09:11.426 --> 00:09:14.763
publishing company, it's
like a Refinery29 competitor.

00:09:14.763 --> 00:09:17.515
I saw the Refinery29 CEO at
CES, and I rolled up on him,

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:20.060
and we've been friends for
a long time, acquaintances,

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:24.189
friendly, and he had a 
good vibe, but I addressed

00:09:24.189 --> 00:09:25.857
it immediately, I'm 
like listen, you know I want

00:09:25.857 --> 00:09:28.693
to rip your head off, like
I'm competitive, it's a game,

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:29.861
like that's what I do.

00:09:29.861 --> 00:09:32.906
I'm like, but then 
I said to him, but honestly,

00:09:32.906 --> 00:09:35.200
I think we should join up 
and we should go after

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.578
Conde Nast and Hearst 
and there's much bigger media

00:09:38.578 --> 00:09:41.539
women companies than us,
together we can really do some

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:44.125
damage, and I don't care if
I get 50 cents or 30 cents

00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:47.128
on the dollar, I think the
best collaborations are the

00:09:47.128 --> 00:09:48.505
most unlikely.

00:09:48.505 --> 00:09:51.132
When Burger King 
asked McDonald's,

00:09:51.132 --> 00:09:53.551
on that campaign 
they did, I don't know

00:09:53.551 --> 00:09:56.471
if you guys saw this, where
they asked to make a love child

00:09:56.471 --> 00:09:58.723
of the Big Mac and the Whopper.

00:09:58.723 --> 00:10:02.227
To me, there's, if 
tomorrow Nike and Adidas,

00:10:02.227 --> 00:10:06.815
in the height of Yeezy's 
going after the Nike brand,

00:10:06.815 --> 00:10:09.651
did a collaboration, 
to me, you even said it,

00:10:09.651 --> 00:10:10.819
the most unlikely ones.

00:10:10.819 --> 00:10:15.615
It's easy when a Crayon 
company does a JV with like

00:10:15.615 --> 00:10:18.159
a sock company and they
make a co-pack like,

00:10:18.159 --> 00:10:20.119
they're not really competing.

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:22.997
I think the most interesting 
ones are when you have

00:10:22.997 --> 00:10:27.168
real genuine competition, 
and people collaborate,

00:10:27.168 --> 00:10:30.421
and so I think the ones that
are most interesting is when

00:10:30.421 --> 00:10:34.217
Facebook and Snapchat will
have secret meetings and say

00:10:34.217 --> 00:10:36.886
you know what, we have to 
team up on vertical video,

00:10:36.886 --> 00:10:40.181
because that's bigger than
what we're competing against

00:10:40.181 --> 00:10:42.767
each other, 'cause we have
to take down television ads.

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:45.436
That's interest, you 
know, I love that stuff.

00:10:45.436 --> 00:10:47.897
I know that's pretty 
frothy, but I think that's

00:10:47.897 --> 00:10:50.149
when it gets most interesting.

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:54.988
So FedEx and UPS should 
get together and say listen,

00:10:54.988 --> 00:10:56.948
Amazon and Uber are dangerous.

00:10:56.948 --> 00:10:58.486
What can we do together?

00:10:58.486 --> 00:10:59.226
- [Woman] So 
disrupt the disruptors.

00:10:59.226 --> 00:11:01.618
Disrupt the disruptors and
it's most interesting to me

00:11:01.618 --> 00:11:03.408
when the incumbents that 
are on the verge of being

00:11:03.408 --> 00:11:06.708
disrupted are 
smart enough to disrupt.

00:11:06.708 --> 00:11:10.962
So like for example, 
every hotel company in the world

00:11:10.962 --> 00:11:13.506
should have gotten together, 
threw money together,

00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:16.483
and then bought residential, 
